  "editorReview": {
    "text": "Tracy O’Neill’s novel shines brightest in the fluorescent hush of the Manchester telephone exchange, where the click of relay racks feels like a secret language. The fair-play mystery is meticulously constructed, and Ruth Calloway’s moral descent offers genuine grit. However, the prose suffers from repetitive tics—the mantra 'The ledger doesn't lie' loses its punch after the seventh recitation—and the middle chapters stall with rhythmic monotony. While the sensory precision of Chapter 9’s theft sequence is immersive, the manuscript too often tells us the tension rather than showing it. A rigor",
    "penName": "疏影",
    "title": "Fiction Editor",
    "rating": "Worth reading"
  },
